From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sun, 06 Sep 2026 01:10:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen/gntdev: return a dma-buf's frame numbers to userspace

A device model that shares a buffer with the guest it serves needs the frame
numbers of that buffer's pages, so it can map them into the guest's physical map
with XENMAPSPACE_gmfn_foreign. For a dma-buf it cannot work them out itself: the
mapping is VM_PFNMAP, which Linux's page walker reports as a hole, so
/proc/self/pagemap returns zeroes and MADV_POPULATE_WRITE -- which is what the
device model uses, because a READ would map the shared zero page -- refuses the
VMA with EINVAL. Measured on a Raspberry Pi 5, where this is what stops a guest from using
Vulkan through virtio-gpu/Venus: the large mappable allocations Mesa's venus makes
are dma-buf backed, so their window stays device-model MMIO, and on arm64 an
emulated access from a SIMD instruction is not even decodable by Xen -- it kills
the guest's client.

gntdev-dmabuf already computes exactly those frame numbers on the import path: it
attaches the dma-buf, maps the attachment, and walks the sg table's dma addresses
to build a gfn array, which it then converts into grant references. Add an ioctl
that hands the array back instead, for a caller that is going to do the mapping
itself rather than pass grant references to the guest:

  IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_GFNS

No grant references are created. The import is registered exactly as IMP_TO_REFS
registers it, so the attachment -- and with it the pages -- stays in place until
IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_RELEASE is called for the same fd, which is a stronger
guarantee than an mlock() the device model could manage on its own.

dmabuf_imp_to_refs() gains an out_gfns argument to serve both callers rather than
duplicating the attach/map/walk sequence; passing NULL keeps the existing
behaviour, which is what the IMP_TO_REFS ioctl does. The grant-reference array
dmabuf_imp_alloc_storage() allocates stays filled with INVALID_GRANT_REF on the
out_gfns path, which is what makes the shared release path
(dmabuf_imp_end_foreign_access()) a no-op for these imports.

The reserved[] words are rejected unless zero, and the struct's size and the
offset of its array are pinned with static_assert: the ioctl number encodes
sizeof(the struct), and the device model on the other side of this interface
carries the same two assertions against its own copy of it, so a layout change on
either side fails a build instead of turning the request into a different ioctl at
run time.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [needs discussion upstream: the natural question
 is whether this belongs in gntdev at all or wants its own interface, and
 whether the frame numbers should be handed out under a capability check]
---
--- a/drivers/xen/gntdev-dmabuf.c
+++ b/drivers/xen/gntdev-dmabuf.c
@@ -554,9 +554,15 @@ fail_no_free:
 	return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Import a dma-buf and either hand back grant references to its pages (@out_gfns
+ * NULL, the IMP_TO_REFS behaviour) or the frame numbers themselves (@out_gfns
+ * non-NULL, for a caller that maps them into another domain's physmap itself).
+ * Either way the attachment is kept, so the pages stay put, until released.
+ */
 static struct gntdev_dmabuf *
 dmabuf_imp_to_refs(struct gntdev_dmabuf_priv *priv, struct device *dev,
-		   int fd, int count, int domid)
+		   int fd, int count, int domid, u64 *out_gfns)
 {
 	struct gntdev_dmabuf *gntdev_dmabuf, *ret;
 	struct dma_buf *dma_buf;
@@ -632,12 +638,18 @@ dmabuf_imp_to_refs(struct gntdev_dmabuf_priv *priv, struct device *dev,
 		gfns[i++] = pfn_to_gfn(pfn);
 	}
 
-	ret = ERR_PTR(dmabuf_imp_grant_foreign_access(gfns,
-						      gntdev_dmabuf->u.imp.refs,
-						      count, domid));
-	kfree(gfns);
-	if (IS_ERR(ret))
-		goto fail_end_access;
+	if (out_gfns) {
+		for (i = 0; i < count; i++)
+			out_gfns[i] = gfns[i];
+		kfree(gfns);
+	} else {
+		ret = ERR_PTR(dmabuf_imp_grant_foreign_access(gfns,
+							     gntdev_dmabuf->u.imp.refs,
+							     count, domid));
+		kfree(gfns);
+		if (IS_ERR(ret))
+			goto fail_end_access;
+	}
 
 	pr_debug("Imported DMA buffer with fd %d\n", fd);
 
@@ -787,7 +799,7 @@ long gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_to_refs(struct gntdev_priv *priv,
 
 	gntdev_dmabuf = dmabuf_imp_to_refs(priv->dmabuf_priv,
 					   priv->dma_dev, op.fd,
-					   op.count, op.domid);
+					   op.count, op.domid, NULL);
 	if (IS_ERR(gntdev_dmabuf))
 		return PTR_ERR(gntdev_dmabuf);
 
@@ -803,6 +815,63 @@ out_release:
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The ioctl NUMBER encodes sizeof(the struct), so userspace and this file have to
+ * agree on the layout or the request silently becomes a different one and the
+ * kernel answers ENOTTY. The device model that uses this carries the same two
+ * assertions against its own copy of the struct, so a change on either side fails
+ * a build rather than an ioctl.
+ */
+static_assert(sizeof(struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns) == 24);
+static_assert(offsetof(struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns, gfns) == 16);
+
+long gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns(struct gntdev_priv *priv,
+				     struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns __user *u)
+{
+	struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns op;
+	struct gntdev_dmabuf *gntdev_dmabuf;
+	u64 *gfns;
+	long ret;
+
+	if (copy_from_user(&op, u, sizeof(op)) != 0)
+		return -EFAULT;
+
+	/*
+	 * "Reserved - must be zero" only means that if it is enforced; otherwise
+	 * the words are decoration and can never carry a future flag.
+	 */
+	if (op.reserved[0] || op.reserved[1])
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	if (unlikely(gntdev_test_page_count(op.count)))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	gfns = kcalloc(op.count, sizeof(*gfns), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!gfns)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	/* No grants are created here, so the domid argument is not used. */
+	gntdev_dmabuf = dmabuf_imp_to_refs(priv->dmabuf_priv,
+					   priv->dma_dev, op.fd,
+					   op.count, 0, gfns);
+	if (IS_ERR(gntdev_dmabuf)) {
+		ret = PTR_ERR(gntdev_dmabuf);
+		goto out_free;
+	}
+
+	if (copy_to_user(u->gfns, gfns, sizeof(*gfns) * op.count) != 0) {
+		ret = -EFAULT;
+		dmabuf_imp_release(priv->dmabuf_priv, op.fd);
+		goto out_free;
+	}
+
+	ret = 0;
+
+out_free:
+	kfree(gfns);
+	return ret;
+}
+
 long gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_release(struct gntdev_priv *priv,
 				     struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_release __user *u)
 {
--- a/drivers/xen/gntdev-dmabuf.h
+++ b/drivers/xen/gntdev-dmabuf.h
@@ -27,6 +27,9 @@ long gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_exp_wait_released(struct gntdev_priv *priv,
 long gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_to_refs(struct gntdev_priv *priv,
 				     struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_refs __user *u);
 
+long gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns(struct gntdev_priv *priv,
+				     struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns __user *u);
+
 long gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_release(struct gntdev_priv *priv,
 				     struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_release __user *u);
 
--- a/drivers/xen/gntdev.c
+++ b/drivers/xen/gntdev.c
@@ -1045,6 +1045,9 @@ static long gntdev_ioctl(struct file *flip,
 	case IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_REFS:
 		return gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_to_refs(priv, ptr);
 
+	case IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_GFNS:
+		return gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns(priv, ptr);
+
 	case IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_RELEASE:
 		return gntdev_ioctl_dmabuf_imp_release(priv, ptr);
 #endif
--- a/include/uapi/xen/gntdev.h
+++ b/include/uapi/xen/gntdev.h
@@ -298,10 +298,42 @@ struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_refs {
 	__u32 refs[1];
 };
 
+/*
+ * Import a dma-buf with file descriptor @fd and return the frame numbers of
+ * its pages in array @gfns of size @count.
+ *
+ * This is the sibling of IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_REFS for a caller that maps
+ * those frames into another domain's physical map itself -- a device model
+ * sharing a buffer with the guest it serves, via XENMAPSPACE_gmfn_foreign --
+ * rather than handing grant references to the guest. A device model cannot work
+ * the frame numbers out for itself: a dma-buf mapping is VM_PFNMAP, which
+ * /proc/self/pagemap reports as a hole.
+ *
+ * No grant references are created. As with IMP_TO_REFS the buffer stays
+ * attached, and so its pages stay where they are, until
+ * IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_RELEASE is called for the same @fd.
+ */
+#define IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_GFNS \
+	_IOC(_IOC_NONE, 'G', 13, \
+	     sizeof(struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns))
+struct ioctl_gntdev_dmabuf_imp_to_gfns {
+	/* IN parameters. */
+	/* File descriptor of the dma-buf. */
+	__u32 fd;
+	/* Number of frame numbers in @gfns array. */
+	__u32 count;
+	/* Reserved - must be zero. */
+	__u32 reserved[2];
+	/* OUT parameters. */
+	/* Array of frame numbers of size @count. */
+	__u64 gfns[1];
+};
+
 /*
  * This will close all references to the imported buffer with file descriptor
  * @fd, so it can be released by the owner. This is only valid for buffers
- * created with IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_REFS.
+ * created with IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_REFS or
+ * IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_TO_GFNS.
  */
 #define IOCTL_GNTDEV_DMABUF_IMP_RELEASE \
 	_IOC(_IOC_NONE, 'G', 12, \
